<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-inverse-variation" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Inverse Variation</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        1.0 Numbers and Algebra
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        1.5 Formulae and Variations
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title> 
                    <p>
                            <ul>
                              <li>Solve direct and inverse variations from real life situations</li>
                              <li>Draw graphs of direct and inverse variations</li>
                            </ul>
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        11
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

<axiom component="lesson-plan">
    <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
    <dl>
        <li>
            <title>Suggested Time</title>
            <p>
                15 minutes
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <title>Objective</title>
            <p>
                To enable learners to discover inverse variation by investigating how changing the number of workers affects the time needed to complete a job while maintaining a constant total of man-hours.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <title>Teacher's Role</title>
            <p>
                Organize learners into groups of 3 to 4. Guide them through the scheduling scenario, helping them record times and identify numerical patterns. Use probing questions to help learners notice that multiplying workers by hours always equals the same number. Encourage discussion and prediction before introducing formal inverse variation definitions.
            </p>
        </li>
    </dl>
</axiom>

<p>To transition from direct variation to <em>Inverse Variation</em>, we move from relationships that grow together to relationships that "balance" each other. This experience uses a real-world scheduling task to help learners discover that as one value increases, the other must decrease to maintain a constant product.</p>

<activity>
    <statement>
        <title>Learner Experience 1.2.1: The Moving Company Challenge</title>

        <p><strong>Group Work:</strong> Form groups of 3 to 4 students for this activity.</p>

        <p><em>Objective:</em> Discover how changing the number of workers changes the time it takes to finish a job.</p>

        <p>Imagine your group runs a moving company. It takes <strong>2 workers exactly 12 hours</strong> to completely clear out a house. If you bring in more workers, the job gets done faster!</p>
        
        <p>The relationship between the number of workers (w) and the time (t) it takes is an <strong>inverse variation</strong>. This means as one goes up, the other goes down.</p>

        <ol>
            <li><strong>Find the constant (k):</strong> Multiply the starting workers by the hours: 
                <p>2 × 12 = 24</p> 
                This "magic number" (24) is your constant. It represents the total "man-hours" needed for the job.
            </li>
            <li><strong>Use the constant to find new times:</strong> To find the time for any number of workers, divide the constant by the number of workers: 
                <p>t = 24 / w</p>
            </li>
        </ol>

        <p>Work together to fill in the missing numbers in the scheduling table below:</p>

        <table>
            <tabular>
                <row header="yes">
                    <cell>Scenario</cell>
                    <cell>Workers (w)</cell>
                    <cell>Time in Hours (t)</cell>
                    <cell>Constant (w × t = k)</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>A</cell>
                    <cell>2</cell>
                    <cell>12</cell>
                    <cell>2 × 12 = 24</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>B</cell>
                    <cell>4</cell>
                    <cell>?</cell>
                    <cell>24</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>C</cell>
                    <cell>6</cell>
                    <cell>?</cell>
                    <cell>24</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>D</cell>
                    <cell>8</cell>
                    <cell>?</cell>
                    <cell>24</cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>Discuss these questions with your group:</p>
        <ol>
            <li>What happens to the time required as you add more workers?</li>
            <li>If a client absolutely needs the house cleared in just 1 hour, how many workers must your company send?</li>
            <li>What do you notice when you multiply the number of workers by the hours in every row?</li>
        </ol>
    </statement>
</activity>

<axiom component="lesson-plan">
    <title>Structured Instruction</title>
    <p>
        <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>
                    10 minutes
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    To formalize learners' discoveries by introducing the definition, equation, graphical representation, and applications of inverse variation.
                </p>
            </li>
        </dl>
    </p>
    <paragraphs>
        <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
        <ol>
            <li>
                <p>
                    <alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> Point back to the moving table. Highlight that while workers increased and hours decreased, their product stayed constant at 24.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    <strong>The Formula:</strong> Introduce y = k/x (or xy = k), where k is the constant.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    <strong>The Rule:</strong> When one value doubles, the other halves.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    <strong>The Graph:</strong> Show that the graph forms a curve called a hyperbola and never touches the origin or axes.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    <strong>Real-World Links:</strong> Connect to scenarios like speed vs. time or pressure vs. volume.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ol>
    </paragraphs>
</axiom>

<insight>
    <p>Inverse variation occurs when the product of two variables is a constant. Mathematically, we express this relationship as y = k/x or xy = k, where y and x are the variables and k is the constant of proportionality.</p>
    <p>Unlike direct variation, the graph of an inverse relationship is never a straight line. It forms a curve known as a hyperbola.</p>
    <figure xml:id="fig-inverse-curve">
        <caption>The inverse variation curve for <m>y = \frac{100}{x}</m>, showing how y falls as x increases.</caption>
        <image width="100%">
            <latex-image>
                \begin{tikzpicture}
                \begin{axis}[
                    grid=both,
                    minor grid style={draw=gray!15, line width=0.2pt},
                    major grid style={draw=gray!40, line width=0.4pt},
                    minor tick num=1,
                    xtick distance=1,
                    ytick distance=10,
                    axis lines=left,
                    ylabel={y},
                    xlabel={x},
                    xmin=0, xmax=11, ymin=0, ymax=110,
                    width=6.5cm, height=7cm,
                    tick label style={font=\small},
                    label style={font=\small}
                ]
                \addplot[red, samples=200, domain=0.5:11] {100/x};
                \addplot[only marks, mark=*, blue, mark size=2pt]
                    coordinates {(1,100) (2,50) (4,25) (5,20) (10,10)};
                \end{axis}
                \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
        </image>
    </figure>
    <ul>
        <li>The curve gets closer and closer to the x and y axes but never actually touches them.</li>
        <li>As x increases, the drop in y is sharp at first and then becomes more gradual.</li>
        <li>The graph never passes through (0,0) because you cannot divide by zero.</li>
    </ul>
    
    <term>Difference Between Direct and Inverse Variation</term>
    
    <tabular top="medium" bottom="medium" left="medium" right="medium">
        <row header="yes">
            <cell>Feature</cell>
            <cell>Direct Variation</cell>
            <cell>Inverse Variation</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell>Equation</cell>
            <cell>y = kx</cell>
            <cell>y = k/x</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell>Relation</cell>
            <cell>y/x = k (Constant Ratio)</cell>
            <cell>xy = k (Constant Product)</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell>Graph</cell>
            <cell>Straight line through origin</cell>
            <cell>Curve (Hyperbola)</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell>Visual Trend</cell>
            <cell>Both go up together</cell>
            <cell>One goes up, the other goes down</cell>
        </row>
    </tabular>
    
    <p>In inverse variation, the total amount (k) is fixed. If you want to change one factor, the other must adapt to keep that total the same.</p>
</insight>

 <p>A classic real-world example is the relationship between speed s and time t taken to cover a fixed distance d:</p>
    <p>s = d/t</p>
    <p>Comparing to y = k/x gives:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>Speed s is the y variable.</li>
        <li>Time t is the x variable.</li>
        <li>Distance d is the constant k.</li>
    </ul>
    <figure xml:id="fig-inverse-speed-time">
        <caption>An inverse variation graph for a fixed distance of 100 km, plotting speed against time.</caption>
        <image width="100%">
            <latex-image>
                \begin{tikzpicture}
                \begin{axis}[
                    grid=both,
                    minor grid style={draw=gray!15, line width=0.2pt},
                    major grid style={draw=gray!40, line width=0.4pt},
                    minor tick num=1,
                    xtick distance=1,
                    ytick distance=10,
                    axis lines=left,
                    ylabel={Speed ($s$) in km/h},
                    xlabel={Time ($t$) in h},
                    xmin=0, xmax=10, ymin=0, ymax=110,
                    width=6.5cm, height=7cm,
                    tick label style={font=\small},
                    label style={font=\small}
                ]
                \addplot[red, samples=200, domain=0.5:10] {100/x};
                \addplot[only marks, mark=*, blue, mark size=2pt]
                    coordinates {(1,100) (2,50) (4,25) (5,20) (10,10)};
                \end{axis}
                \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
        </image>
    </figure>

    <p>For a fixed distance of 100 km:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>At 100 km/h, it takes 1 hour. (100 × 1 = 100)</li>
        <li>At 50 km/h, it takes 2 hours. (50 × 2 = 100)</li>
        <li>At 25 km/h, it takes 4 hours. (25 × 4 = 100)</li>
    </ul>
    <p>As the speed halves, the time doubles. The product remains 100.</p>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>It is known that <m>y \propto \frac{1}{x}</m>. If <m>y = 20</m> when <m>x = 3</m>, find <m>y</m> when <m>x = \frac{1}{2}</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <ol>
                <li><p>Find the constant (<m>k</m>): <m>y = \frac{k}{x} \implies k = y \times x</m>. <m>k = 20 \times 3 = 60</m>.</p></li>
                <li><p>Find <m>y</m>: <m>y = \frac{60}{1/2}</m> <m>y = 60 \times 2</m></p>
                    <p><m>y = 120</m></p></li>
            </ol>
            <figure xml:id="fig-inverse-example-graph">
                <caption>Graph of the inverse variation <m>y = \frac{60}{x}</m> showing key points.</caption>
                <image width="100%">
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}
                        \begin{axis}[
                            grid=both,
                            minor grid style={draw=gray!15, line width=0.2pt},
                            major grid style={draw=gray!40, line width=0.4pt},
                            minor tick num=1,
                            xtick distance=1,
                            ytick distance=10,
                            axis lines=left,
                            ylabel={y},
                            xlabel={x},
                            xmin=0, xmax=10, ymin=0, ymax=70,
                            width=6.5cm, height=7cm,
                            tick label style={font=\small},
                            label style={font=\small}
                        ]
                        \addplot[red, samples=100, domain=0.5:10] {60/x};
                        \addplot[only marks, mark=*, blue, mark size=2pt] coordinates {(1,60) (2,30) (3,20) (4,15) (5,12) (6,10) (10,6)};
                        \end{axis}
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>Given that <m>p \propto \frac{1}{q}</m> and that when <m>p = 8, q = 25</m>, find:</p>
            <p>(a) the equation connecting <m>p</m> and <m>q</m>.</p>
            <p>(b) <m>p</m> when <m>q = 10</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>(a) <m>k = p \times q = 8 \times 25 = 200</m>.</p>
            <p>Equation: <m>p = \frac{200}{q}</m></p>
            <p>(b) <m>p = \frac{200}{10}</m></p>
            <p><m>p = 20</m></p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>A quantity <m>y</m> varies inversely as a quantity <m>x</m>. Copy and complete the table:</p>
            <tabular halign="center">
                <row header="yes">
                    <cell><m>x</m></cell>
                    <cell>2</cell>
                    <cell>4</cell>
                    <cell>8</cell>
                    <cell>16</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><m>y</m></cell>
                    <cell>16</cell>
                    <cell>—</cell>
                    <cell>—</cell>
                    <cell>2</cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
            <p>(a) Find the constant of proportionality.</p>
            <p>(b) Describe the shape of the graph if <m>y</m> is plotted against <m>x</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <ol>
                <li><p>Constant: Using <m>(2, 16)</m>, <m>k = 2 \times 16 = 32</m>.</p></li>
                <li><p>Missing values: For <m>x=4, y=32/4 = 8</m>. For <m>x=8, y=32/8 = 4</m>.</p></li>
                
                <li><p>Graph Shape: A plot of <m>y</m> against <m>x</m> forms a <em>rectangular hyperbola</em> (a smooth curve that approaches the axes but never touches them).</p></li>
            </ol>
            <figure xml:id="fig-inverse-table-graph">
                <caption>Graph of <m>y = \frac{32}{x}</m> for the example values in the table.</caption>
                <image width="100%">
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}
                        \begin{axis}[
                            grid=both,
                            minor grid style={draw=gray!15, line width=0.2pt},
                            major grid style={draw=gray!40, line width=0.4pt},
                            minor tick num=1,
                            xtick distance=2,
                            ytick distance=4,
                            axis lines=left,
                            ylabel={y},
                            xlabel={x},
                            xmin=0, xmax=18, ymin=0, ymax=18,
                            width=6.5cm, height=7cm,
                            tick label style={font=\small},
                            label style={font=\small}
                        ]
                        \addplot[red, samples=200, domain=0.5:16] {32/x};
                        \addplot[only marks, mark=*, blue, mark size=2pt] coordinates {(2,16) (4,8) (8,4) (16,2)};
                        \end{axis}
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>The data below follows the relationship <m>y = \frac{k}{x}</m>.</p>
            <tabular halign="center">
                <row header="yes">
                    <cell><m>x</m></cell>
                    <cell>1</cell>
                    <cell>2</cell>
                    <cell>5</cell>
                    <cell>10</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><m>1/x</m></cell>
                    <cell>1.0</cell>
                    <cell>0.5</cell>
                    <cell>0.2</cell>
                    <cell>0.1</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><m>y</m></cell>
                    <cell>50</cell>
                    <cell>25</cell>
                    <cell>10</cell>
                    <cell>5</cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
            <p>If you were to draw a graph of <m>y</m> against <m>\frac{1}{x}</m>, what would be the gradient (slope) of the resulting line?</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <ol>
                <li><p>Relationship: The equation <m>y = \frac{k}{x}</m> can be written as <m>y = k(\frac{1}{x})</m>.</p></li>
                <li><p>Identify <m>k</m>: <m>k = y \times x = 50 \times 1 = 50</m>.</p></li>
                <li><p>Interpretation: When <m>y</m> is plotted against <m>\frac{1}{x}</m>, the graph is a straight line through the origin with gradient <m>k</m>.</p>
                    <p>The gradient is 50.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>The pressure (<m>P</m>) of a gas varies inversely as the volume (<m>V</m>). When <m>V = 10, P = 2</m>.</p>
            <p>(a) Find the equation relating <m>P</m> and <m>V</m>.</p>
            <p>(b) If the volume is doubled, what happens to the pressure? Show this using the graph.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>(a) <m>k = 10 \times 2 = 20</m>. Equation: <m>P = \frac{20}{V}</m></p>
            <p>(b) If <m>V</m> doubles (e.g., from 10 to 20):</p>
            <p><m>P = \frac{20}{20} = 1</m>.</p>
            <p>The pressure is halved.</p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-inverse-pressure-volume">
                <caption>Inverse variation graph of <m>P = \frac{20}{V}</m>, showing pressure versus volume.</caption>
                <image width="100%">
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}
                        \begin{axis}[
                            grid=both,
                            minor grid style={draw=gray!15, line width=0.2pt},
                            major grid style={draw=gray!40, line width=0.4pt},
                            minor tick num=1,
                            xtick distance=2,
                            ytick distance=2,
                            axis lines=left,
                            ylabel={Pressure ($P$)},
                            xlabel={Volume ($V$)},
                            xmin=0, xmax=22, ymin=0, ymax=12,
                            width=6.5cm, height=7cm,
                            tick label style={font=\small},
                            label style={font=\small}
                        ]
                        \addplot[red, samples=200, domain=0.5:20] {20/x};
                        \addplot[only marks, mark=*, blue, mark size=2pt] coordinates {(10,2) (20,1)};
                        \end{axis}
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
            <p>On the graph, as you move further right along the x-axis (Volume), the y-value (Pressure) drops proportionally toward the axis.</p>
        </solution>
    </example>

